Abstract

This specification defines the Cognitive Domain Model of the Memory Operating System (MOS).

A Cognitive Domain is not a storage location, memory type, database schema, or physical partition.

Instead, a Cognitive Domain is a policy-governed semantic namespace over the Cognitive State Space.

Domains organize cognition according to semantic purpose while preserving a single canonical representation of every Cognitive Element.

A Cognitive Element SHALL exist exactly once within the Cognitive State Space and MAY participate in zero or more Cognitive Domains through policy-governed semantic projection.

1. Purpose

RFC-0001 defines the principles of cognition.

RFC-0002 defines cognitive evolution.

RFC-0003 defines the structure of cognition.

RFC-0004 defines how cognition is organized.

Organization SHALL remain independent from storage implementation.

2. Domain Axiom

MOS defines the following organizational principle:

A Cognitive Domain SHALL organize cognition through semantic policies rather than physical ownership.

Domains SHALL NOT own Cognitive Elements.

Domains SHALL expose semantic views over the Cognitive State Space.

3. The Domain Model

A Cognitive Domain is composed of five normative components:

Identity
Purpose
Membership Policies
Behavioral Policies
Optimization Policies

Every compliant implementation SHALL preserve these semantics regardless of storage architecture.

4. Identity

Every Cognitive Domain SHALL possess a stable identity.

Identity represents the semantic purpose of the domain.

Domain identity SHALL remain independent from:

implementation
storage
deployment
network topology
programming language
5. Purpose

Every domain SHALL define exactly one primary semantic purpose.

Examples include:

knowledge organization
attention management
planning
execution
reflection
governance

Purpose defines intent, not implementation.

6. Membership Policies

Membership SHALL be determined exclusively through policy evaluation.

A Membership Policy specifies the conditions under which a Cognitive Element participates in a domain.

Example (illustrative only):

confidence ≥ 0.85
AND validated = true
AND archived = false

Membership SHALL be:

deterministic
explainable
reproducible
version-aware

Elements SHALL NOT be copied into domains.

7. Behavioral Policies

Behavioral Policies define how a domain influences cognition.

Examples include:

retrieval priority
activation behavior
retention behavior
visibility
reasoning scope
planning participation

Behavioral Policies SHALL NOT modify element identity.

8. Optimization Policies

Optimization Policies define implementation-independent performance goals.

Examples include:

preferred indexing
caching eligibility
precomputation
materialization hints
archival preference

These policies describe desired behavior without prescribing implementation.

8.1 Policy Elements

MOS defines Policies as first-class Cognitive Elements.

Policies SHALL participate in the Cognitive State Space using the same semantic model as every other Cognitive Element.

A Policy SHALL possess:

Identity
Purpose
Version History
Provenance
Relationships
Lifecycle
Explainability

Policies SHALL NOT be represented as opaque implementation configuration when they influence canonical cognition.

Policy Axiom

MOS defines the following rule.

Every rule governing cognition SHALL itself be representable as cognition.

This enables the Memory Operating System to reason about, explain, audit, version, and improve its own governing behavior.

Policy Categories

RFC-0004 defines several canonical policy categories.

Membership Policies

Determine whether a Cognitive Element belongs to a Cognitive Domain.

Examples:

confidence threshold
validation requirements
archival status
trust level

Membership Policies determine visibility.

They SHALL NOT modify Cognitive Elements.

Behavioral Policies

Determine how a domain behaves.

Examples:

retrieval priority
activation strategy
retention strategy
reasoning scope
planning participation

Behavioral Policies influence cognition without changing identity.

Optimization Policies

Describe optimization objectives.

Examples include:

cache eligibility
preferred indexing
pre-computed projections
materialization
archival preference

Optimization Policies SHALL preserve semantic equivalence.

Governance Policies

Govern the Cognitive State Space itself.

Examples:

authorization
compliance
trust
security
privacy
organizational rules

Governance Policies SHALL be evaluated before any state-modifying transaction.

Learning Policies

Govern adaptation.

Learning Policies determine:

when learning occurs
acceptable evidence
confidence adjustments
policy evolution
consolidation strategies

Learning Policies SHALL preserve historical explainability.

Policy Relationships

Policies MAY reference:

Domains
Cognitive Elements
Capabilities
Functions/strategy
Operations
Transactions
Events
Other Policies

Policies therefore become part of the semantic graph.

Policy Versioning

Policies SHALL evolve through normal version evolution.

Changing a policy SHALL NOT overwrite previous policy versions.

Historical decisions SHALL remain explainable using the policy version active at the time.

Policy Explainability

Every policy evaluation SHALL be explainable.

A compliant implementation SHALL reconstruct:

which policy was evaluated
which version was used
which conditions matched
which conditions failed
resulting decision
producing transaction
Policy Composition

Policies MAY be composed.

Example:

Membership Policy

AND

Security Policy

AND

Trust Policy

AND

Project Policy

The resulting policy SHALL behave as a single logical policy while preserving explainability for each constituent policy.

Policy Lifecycle

Policies follow the same lifecycle as every Cognitive Element.

Create

↓

Validate

↓

Activate

↓

Observe

↓

Refine

↓

Version

↓

Supersede

↓

Archive

Policies SHALL NEVER be silently replaced.

I think we should introduce a concept called Meta-Cognition.

Policies are only the first example.

Eventually, MOS will contain other objects that describe, govern, or evaluate cognition itself:

Policies
Constraints
Ontologies
Taxonomies
Schemas
Trust Models
Capability Definitions
Domain Definitions
Evaluation Metrics

These don't represent the outside world—they represent the cognitive system itself.

Rather than treating each as a special case, I'd define them collectively as Meta-Cognitive Elements: Cognitive Elements whose primary purpose is to describe, constrain, organize, or govern other Cognitive Elements.

This gives us another elegant layer in the architecture:

Reality
        │
        ▼
Cognitive Elements
        │
        ▼
Cognitive Domains
        │
        ▼
Meta-Cognitive Elements
        │
        ▼
Policies
Constraints
Ontologies
Capability Definitions
Trust Models

That single abstraction will simplify many later RFCs because we won't need separate rules for every kind of "system knowledge." They'll all inherit the same semantics as first-class Meta-Cognitive Elements. I think this is the last major conceptual piece needed before RFC-0004 can be considered stable and before we revisit RFC-0005 with an even cleaner foundation.

9. Domain Membership

A Cognitive Element MAY participate in:

zero domains
one domain
many domains

Participation SHALL NOT create duplicate cognition.

Membership SHALL be evaluated dynamically according to policy.

10. Dynamic Projection

Domains are semantic projections.

Membership MAY change without modifying the underlying Cognitive Element.

Example:

Confidence decreases

↓

Membership Policy evaluates to false

↓

Element is no longer visible in the Knowledge Domain

The element itself remains unchanged.

11. Domain Composition

Domains MAY be composed.

Example:

Knowledge
AND
Financial
AND
Prediction

The resulting composed domain represents the intersection of participating policies.

Composition SHALL NOT duplicate Cognitive Elements.

12. Canonical Domains

RFC-0004 defines the minimum semantic domains recognized by MOS.

These domains define organization, not storage.

12.1 Observation Domain

Purpose:

Represent newly acquired observations awaiting interpretation.

Characteristics:

high ingestion
transient prominence
uncertain knowledge
12.2 Knowledge Domain

Purpose:

Represent validated cognitive knowledge.

Membership typically depends upon:

confidence
validation
provenance
policy compliance
12.3 Attention Domain

Purpose:

Represent currently active cognition.

Attention is computed dynamically.

Attention SHALL NOT require dedicated storage.

12.4 Planning Domain

Purpose:

Represent intended future cognition.

Includes:

goals
tasks
dependencies
milestones
12.5 Execution Domain

Purpose:

Represent interactions with external systems.

Execution produces new observations.

12.6 Reflection Domain

Purpose:

Continuously improve cognition.

Typical activities:

consolidation
contradiction analysis
summarization
confidence recalibration
12.7 Learning Domain

Purpose:

Represent adaptations that improve future cognitive behavior.

Learning SHALL preserve historical provenance.

12.8 Prediction Domain

Purpose:

Represent hypotheses regarding future states.

Predictions SHALL remain distinguishable from observations.

12.9 Governance Domain

Purpose:

Represent policies governing cognition itself.

Includes:

authorization
trust
compliance
security
ethical constraints
organizational policies
12.10 Archive Domain

Purpose:

Represent cognition with low current activation.

Archive SHALL NOT imply deletion.

Archived cognition SHALL remain addressable and explainable.

13. Domain Lifecycle

Domains evolve independently from Cognitive Elements.

A domain MAY:

change policies
change optimization strategies
introduce new behavioral semantics

Such changes SHALL NOT invalidate historical provenance.

14. Extensibility

Implementations MAY define additional domains.

Custom domains SHALL:

possess unique identities
define explicit policies
preserve MOS semantic guarantees

Existing domains SHALL NOT be redefined incompatibly.

15. Explainability

For every domain membership decision, a compliant implementation SHALL be able to explain:

why an element belongs to the domain
which policies were evaluated
which conditions were satisfied or rejected
when the evaluation occurred

Domain participation SHALL always be explainable.

16. Compliance

An implementation MAY claim compliance with RFC-0004 only if it:

maintains a single canonical Cognitive State Space,
represents domains as policy-governed semantic namespaces,
evaluates membership through explicit policies,
preserves explainability,
avoids duplicating canonical cognition.
